		<item>
		<title>Key Takeaways from President Williams’s Interview with The New York Times</title>
		<link>https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/2025/10/10/key-takeaways-from-president-williamss-interview-with-the-new-york-times-2/</link>
		
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Brian Manning]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Fri, 10 Oct 2025 13:19:20 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[Economic Outlook]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/?p=4205</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[On Thursday, October 9, <em>The New York Times</em> published a <a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2025/10/09/us/politics/new-york-fed-president-williams-interview-interest-rate-cuts.html?unlocked_article_code=1.sE8.C7fC.nySAsDR820yv">transcript</a> of an interview with New York Fed President John Williams. In it, he discussed the labor market, inflation, his monetary policy outlook, and central bank independence.]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p class="ts-blog-article-author">
    Brian Manning</p>



<figure class="lse-featured-image">
	<img decoding="async" loading="eager" width="920" height="576" src="https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2025/10/ttw-john-williams-speaking-920x576-1.jpg?quality=80&amp;w=920" class="cover-image asset-image img-responsive wp-post-image" alt="ttw-john-williams-speaking-920x576" post_index="0" srcset="https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2025/10/ttw-john-williams-speaking-920x576-1.jpg 920w, https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2025/10/ttw-john-williams-speaking-920x576-1.jpg?resize=300,188 300w, https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2025/10/ttw-john-williams-speaking-920x576-1.jpg?resize=768,481 768w" sizes="(max-width: 920px) 100vw, 920px" /></figure>



<p>On Thursday, October 9, <em>The New York Times</em> published a <a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2025/10/09/us/politics/new-york-fed-president-williams-interview-interest-rate-cuts.html?unlocked_article_code=1.sE8.C7fC.nySAsDR820yv">transcript</a> of an interview with New York Fed President John Williams. In it, he discussed the labor market, inflation, his monetary policy outlook, and central bank independence.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">He said:</p>



<blockquote class="wp-block-quote is-layout-flow wp-block-quote-is-layout-flow">
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">“The risks of a further slowdown in the labor market [are] something I’m very focused on.”</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">“We have succeeded in bringing inflation down quite a ways, but we need to get the job done. . . . But we need to do it in a way that does our best to minimize the risk of the labor market cooling more sharply.”</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">“[T]he best way to preserve both independence and the credibility of the central bank is to achieve your goals as well as you can.”</p>
</blockquote>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">In the interview, President Williams said that while there is no “risk-free path” to achieving the Fed&#8217;s employment and price stability goals, “the adjustment we’ve made in monetary policy is really designed to broaden that path.”</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">In terms of his outlook, he expects inflation to move up to around 3 percent and for the unemployment rate to move up gradually. “I think there’s more downside risks to the labor market and employment, and that is something that takes some of the upside risk off of inflation,” he said.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">He also discussed central bank independence, pointing out that countries around the world have given their central banks room to carry out their responsibilities while being accountable to elected officials. “What we’ve seen from experience is that independent central banks are better at achieving the goals that the people and the government want to achieve,” he said. “The evidence supports that, especially around avoiding inflation.”</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2025/10/09/us/politics/new-york-fed-president-williams-interview-interest-rate-cuts.html?unlocked_article_code=1.sE8.C7fC.nySAsDR820yv">Read the full transcript.</a></p>



<div>
<div style="height:24px" aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-spacer"></div>



