		<item>
		<title>Key Takeaways from President Williams’s Speech at the 2023 U.S. Treasury Market Conference</title>
		<link>https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/2023/11/16/key-takeaways-from-president-williamss-speech-at-the-2023-u-s-treasury-market-conference/</link>
		
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Julie Lasson]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Thu, 16 Nov 2023 14:29:23 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[Financial Markets]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/?p=2817</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[On Thursday, November 16, 2023, New York Fed President John Williams <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/newsevents/speeches/2023/wil231116">spoke</a> at the <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/newsevents/events/markets/2023/1116-2023">2023 U.S. Treasury Market Conference</a> about how transactions-based, transparent, and well-documented data is critical to the collective understanding and decision-making at the Federal Reserve and beyond.]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p class="ts-blog-article-author">
    Julie Lasson</p>



<figure class="lse-featured-image">
	<img decoding="async" loading="eager" width="920" height="576" src="https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/11/ttw-jcw-keynote-remarks-at-the-2023-US-Treasury-Market-Conference-920x576-option3.jpg?quality=80&amp;w=920" class="cover-image asset-image img-responsive wp-post-image" alt="" post_index="0" srcset="https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/11/ttw-jcw-keynote-remarks-at-the-2023-US-Treasury-Market-Conference-920x576-option3.jpg 920w, https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/11/ttw-jcw-keynote-remarks-at-the-2023-US-Treasury-Market-Conference-920x576-option3.jpg?resize=300,188 300w, https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/11/ttw-jcw-keynote-remarks-at-the-2023-US-Treasury-Market-Conference-920x576-option3.jpg?resize=768,481 768w" sizes="(max-width: 920px) 100vw, 920px" /></figure>



<p>On Thursday, November 16, 2023, New York Fed President John Williams <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/newsevents/speeches/2023/wil231116">spoke</a> at the <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/newsevents/events/markets/2023/1116-2023">2023 U.S. Treasury Market Conference</a> about how transactions-based, transparent, and well-documented data is critical to the collective understanding and decision-making at the Federal Reserve and beyond.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">He said:</p>



<blockquote class="wp-block-quote is-layout-flow wp-block-quote-is-layout-flow">
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">“As we move forward in the post-LIBOR era, it’s important that we continue to prioritize transparency and clarity in data, especially financial market data.”</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">“If data are worth using, it’s absolutely worth the effort to make sure people know what they are getting.”</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">“[T]here is an opportunity for central banks, governments, and the private sector to come together to improve data transparency and accuracy.”</p>
</blockquote>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Given the widespread dependence on data, President Williams emphasized the need to trust and understand the source of data. He noted that, “while we assume that most data are ‘good,’ there are, unfortunately, ‘bad’ data, too.”</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">He pointed to LIBOR as an example. “The lesson of LIBOR is that there are times when what we think of as strong financial market data is only a mirage,” he said.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">President Williams highlighted the New York Fed’s work in areas such as reference rates and market surveys, and emphasized that transparency in financial data must remain a top priority for the industry. “This is particularly true in the age of AI, when the sources of data are harder to trace,” he said.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">He closed by stressing the importance of collaboration between the public and private sectors, as well as regulatory groups, industry groups, and global standard setters, to “think through the opportunities to further develop data transparency and make improvements.”</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/newsevents/speeches/2023/wil231116">Read the full speech.</a></p>



<div style="height:23px" aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-spacer"></div>



<p class="is-style-bio-contact wp-block-paragraph">Julie Lasson is an executive communications specialist at the New York Fed. </p>



