		<item>
		<title>Inspiring the Next Generation of STEM Leaders: Pam Dyson’s Conversation with Black Girls Code</title>
		<link>https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/2019/06/05/inspiring-the-next-generation-of-stem-leaders-pam-dysons-conversation-with-black-girls-code/</link>
		
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Christine Gianakis, Corporate Communications Associate, Communications and Outreach]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Wed, 05 Jun 2019 16:32:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[Diversity & Inclusion]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Economic Education]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/?p=1609</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[At the end of April, the New York Fed hosted an economic education event for about 50 middle and high school students from <a href="http://www.blackgirlscode.com/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">Black Girls Code</a>, a nonprofit organization that focuses on increasing the number of women of color in the STEM fields (science, technology, engineering, and math).]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p class="ts-blog-article-author">
    Christine Gianakis, Corporate Communications Associate, Communications and Outreach</p>



<figure class="lse-featured-image">
	<img decoding="async" loading="eager" width="1024" height="682" src="https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/1_8qqEzWix8npCTOBF83aJLA.webp?quality=80&amp;w=1024" class="cover-image asset-image img-responsive wp-post-image" alt="" post_index="0" srcset="https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/1_8qqEzWix8npCTOBF83aJLA.webp 1100w, https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/1_8qqEzWix8npCTOBF83aJLA.webp?resize=300,200 300w, https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/1_8qqEzWix8npCTOBF83aJLA.webp?resize=768,512 768w, https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/1_8qqEzWix8npCTOBF83aJLA.webp?resize=1024,682 1024w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p>At the end of April, the New York Fed hosted an economic education event for about 50 middle and high school students from <a href="http://www.blackgirlscode.com/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">Black Girls Code</a>, a nonprofit organization that focuses on increasing the number of women of color in the STEM fields (science, technology, engineering, and math).</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="786" height="411" src="https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/1_aK-WXvHCAUu_JPFD02U5oA.webp" alt="" class="wp-image-1612" srcset="https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/1_aK-WXvHCAUu_JPFD02U5oA.webp 786w, https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/1_aK-WXvHCAUu_JPFD02U5oA.webp?resize=300,157 300w, https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/1_aK-WXvHCAUu_JPFD02U5oA.webp?resize=768,402 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 786px) 100vw, 786px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The students participated in a tour of the New York Fed’s museum and gold vault and a mentoring luncheon, where they met women in technology roles in business areas across the New York Fed. Then they had the opportunity to hear about STEM career paths from New York Fed EVP&nbsp;<a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/aboutthefed/orgchart/dyson.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">Pam Dyson</a>, chief information officer and head of the Technology Group. In a moderated discussion, Dyson spoke about her career, the roles and responsibilities of the Federal Reserve System, and professional development tips for STEM career paths.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" height="682" width="1024" src="https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/1_XQW8-cRN-6ja6-31zadLDQ.webp?w=1024" alt="" class="wp-image-1613" srcset="https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/1_XQW8-cRN-6ja6-31zadLDQ.webp 1400w, https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/1_XQW8-cRN-6ja6-31zadLDQ.webp?resize=300,200 300w, https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/1_XQW8-cRN-6ja6-31zadLDQ.webp?resize=768,512 768w, https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/1_XQW8-cRN-6ja6-31zadLDQ.webp?resize=1024,682 1024w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph" id="6204">Dyson opened the conversation with an overview of her experience and career development, joking with the students about how when she first entered technology, “the ‘cloud’ was just something up in the sky.” She noted the importance of planning with respect to career management and professional development. “It’s not too early to have a plan,” she said. “Be bold and aggressive, but make sure your milestones are achievable.”</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph" id="aaf5">She also shared the best advice she received as a student: “Two things: the first is to be yourself. Bring your authentic self to your leadership role — you may have role models who you look up to and admire, but at the end of the day, you need to be your own self. The second is to lead by example. Never ask your team to do anything you wouldn’t do.”</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="786" height="524" src="https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/1_S67muL-rg8W8u-XkRqVU7g.webp" alt="" class="wp-image-1614" srcset="https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/1_S67muL-rg8W8u-XkRqVU7g.webp 786w, https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/1_S67muL-rg8W8u-XkRqVU7g.webp?resize=300,200 300w, https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/1_S67muL-rg8W8u-XkRqVU7g.webp?resize=768,512 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 786px) 100vw, 786px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph" id="fc15">Responding to a question about her leadership style, Dyson highlighted the importance of teamwork. “Collaboration is very important,” she said. “No individual can move an initiative forward alone.”</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph" id="d571">Dyson emphasized the importance of finding mentors and social groups to build a network of support and explained how mentors can come in many forms. “Ask yourself: who is impactful? As life changes, look for different types of mentors,” she said. “We can use a mentor for every stage of our lives. Include people who don’t look like you, because they’ll help you learn different skillsets and strategies for success.”</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="786" height="411" src="https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/1_ZVAlzlnPWXuLUmxzJ4UJgA.webp" alt="" class="wp-image-1615" srcset="https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/1_ZVAlzlnPWXuLUmxzJ4UJgA.webp 786w, https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/1_ZVAlzlnPWXuLUmxzJ4UJgA.webp?resize=300,157 300w, https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/1_ZVAlzlnPWXuLUmxzJ4UJgA.webp?resize=768,402 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 786px) 100vw, 786px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">What advice did Dyson offer the students as they embark on careers in technology? “Don’t wait for someone else to tell you that you can do something. At some point you just have to jump in — at some point you just need to get in the game.</p>



<div style="height:23px" aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-spacer"></div>



