		<item>
		<title>Comic Books as a Teaching Tool: Making the Language of Economics Universal</title>
		<link>https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/2019/05/03/comic-books-as-a-teaching-tool-making-the-language-of-economics-universal/</link>
		
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Anika Pratt, Assistant Vice President, and Graham Long, Senior Associate, Communications and Outreach]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Fri, 03 May 2019 04:01:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[Economic Education]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/?p=1480</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[“Way, way out — at the edge of the universe — there’s a little planet called Novus.” So began the New York Fed’s return to publishing <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/outreach-and-education/comic-books" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">comic books</a> to promote economic literacy, especially among students from middle school through college.]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p class="ts-blog-article-author">
    Anika Pratt, Assistant Vice President, and Graham Long, Senior Associate, Communications and Outreach</p>



<figure class="lse-featured-image">
	<img decoding="async" loading="eager" width="1024" height="682" src="https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/medium-05032019.png?quality=80&amp;w=1024" class="cover-image asset-image img-responsive wp-post-image" alt="" post_index="0" srcset="https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/medium-05032019.png 1698w, https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/medium-05032019.png?resize=300,200 300w, https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/medium-05032019.png?resize=768,512 768w, https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/medium-05032019.png?resize=1024,682 1024w, https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/medium-05032019.png?resize=1536,1023 1536w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p>“Way, way out — at the edge of the universe — there’s a little planet called Novus.” So began the New York Fed’s return to publishing <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/outreach-and-education/comic-books" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">comic books</a> to promote economic literacy, especially among students from middle school through college.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph" id="2f24">The New York Fed has published educational comic books since the 1950s, and in 2017 we reintroduced the series with an intergalactic theme to help bring core economics lessons to today’s audiences. We recently published the third installment of the new series. These comic books are a resource for educators looking to help students understand economic principles such as employment and inflation, as well as the Federal Reserve’s role in the financial system. Through a playful setting and narratives, they are designed to make complex concepts accessible.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph" id="e810">In the latest issue,&nbsp;<a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/medialibrary/media/outreach-and-education/comic-books/NewYorkFed-TheStoryOfMonetaryPolicy-WebColor.pdf" rel="noreferrer noopener" target="_blank"><em>The Story of Monetary Policy</em></a>, friendly characters from Planet Novus tour the galaxy to advise other worlds on how to conduct monetary policy and help keep economies stable.</p>


<div class="wp-block-image">
<figure class="aligncenter size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" height="1024" width="865" src="https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/comic-book-inflation.png?w=865" alt="" class="wp-image-1482" srcset="https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/comic-book-inflation.png 1203w, https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/comic-book-inflation.png?resize=253,300 253w, https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/comic-book-inflation.png?resize=768,909 768w, https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/comic-book-inflation.png?resize=865,1024 865w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 865px) 100vw, 865px" /></figure>
</div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph" id="2bb6">To accompany each issue in the series, the New York Fed produced classroom&nbsp;<a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/outreach-and-education/comic-books#download" rel="noreferrer noopener" target="_blank">lesson plans</a>&nbsp;for both middle school and high school that build off each story and the experiences of its characters. These lesson plans, which follow the&nbsp;<a href="https://www.socialstudies.org/c3" rel="noreferrer noopener" target="_blank">College, Career, and Civic Life (C3) framework</a>&nbsp;developed by the&nbsp;<a href="https://www.socialstudies.org/" rel="noreferrer noopener" target="_blank">National Council for the Social Studies</a>, challenge students to answer questions about the economy, analyze charts and graphs, read historical texts, create timelines and business proposals, and simulate the function of markets. They include activities, worksheets, and guidelines to promote classroom discussion and encourage students to engage with their communities.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph" id="c832"><em>The Story of Monetary Policy&nbsp;</em>continues the conceit of the previous issues in the new series. The inaugural issue,&nbsp;<a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/medialibrary/media/outreach-and-education/comic-books/NewYorkFed-OnceUponADime-WebFullColor.pdf" rel="noreferrer noopener" target="_blank"><em>Once Upon a Dime</em></a>, introduced the denizens of Planet Novus alongside economic concepts such as barter, currency, and banking. In the second issue,&nbsp;<a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/medialibrary/media/outreach-and-education/comic-books/NewYorkFed-StoryoftheFederalReserveSystem-WebColor.pdf" rel="noreferrer noopener" target="_blank"><em>The Story of the Federal Reserve System</em></a>, these characters visit Earth to help solve a monetary crisis on their home planet.</p>


<div class="wp-block-image">
<figure class="aligncenter size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" height="476" width="1024" src="https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/comic-book-economy.png?w=1024" alt="" class="wp-image-1484" srcset="https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/comic-book-economy.png 2506w, https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/comic-book-economy.png?resize=300,139 300w, https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/comic-book-economy.png?resize=768,357 768w, https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/comic-book-economy.png?resize=1024,476 1024w, https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/comic-book-economy.png?resize=1536,713 1536w, https://tellerwindow.newyorkfed.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2023/06/comic-book-economy.png?resize=2048,951 2048w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>
</div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The comic book series is part of the New York Fed’s broader economic education efforts, including programs and resources for students, teachers, and the public, developed to teach basic economic literacy and increase awareness about the roles and responsibilities of the Federal Reserve System. The three issues with the corresponding lesson plans are available at no cost. Stay tuned for more adventures for the characters from Planet Novus before long.</p>


<div class="wp-block-image">
